00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:06.000
So in a manner of speaking, the internet
is the largest machine that humanity has
ever built, if you go by geographic

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:13.920
extent. The other competitor to that is
the power distribution system, but that
does not span across continents.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:23.100
Nobody's building a transoceanic power
cable. People are. Apparently, they're
trying to get power from Australia to

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.200
Singapore, but whatever.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:37.800
And we take it for granted these days, but
you can literally send data from anywhere
on the planet to anywhere else. And this

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:44.380
is not like one set of systems. There's
not one set of satellites you can go and
send data that exists, but that's not the

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:53.360
main backbone of the internet. This works
through equipment owned by thousands of
different organizations. Like at UT, UT

00:05:53.360 --> 00:06:03.300
owns this Wi-Fi router. It then goes off
into a local ISP, AT&T or whoever, and
then it keeps getting handed off one

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:10.180
organization at a time until it reaches a
destination. And then it comes back. And
all of this and the internet has been

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:17.740
continuously evolving. It supports
basically any application you want. I
mean, the internet started off with file

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:29.040
transfers and emails. Every five or 10
years, this keeps changing and we've come
a long way since then. And the same basic

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:37.700
design has survived despite these decades
of constant evolution. In fact, even every
year of this class, I have to update this

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:39.340
course simply because the world has
changed.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:53.460
It keeps evolving rapidly. And as I said,
this is, in a manner of speaking, the
largest human-built machine.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:04.860
And broadly speaking, the study of
networks, so you can divide computer
science into three rough pieces. The first

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:12.780
piece is theory. So this is the
mathematical foundation of computer
science which asks, what can I compute?

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:19.660
What can I not compute? If I want to
compute something in secret, how do I do
that? That's the cryptography aspect of

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:27.500
it. The second is artificial intelligence,
which in 2026, even non-computer
scientists know exactly what that is. I

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:42.260
mean, what LLMs are, there's more to AI
than just LLMs. And then there is computer
systems. And computer systems, so theory is what

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:55.540
can be computed, as I said. And AI is in a
world where things are not precisely
defined, how do you deal with them? That's

00:07:55.540 --> 00:08:05.480
roughly speaking AI. And computer systems
is part of a broader study of systems. And
the same thing applies in mechanical

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:14.260
engineering, in civil engineering, and
every other form of engineering, where you
have components that you kind of sort of

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:21.340
know how they work, but you still need to
put them together to get what you want.
Your laptop is an incredibly complicated

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:29.720
piece of machine. Basically, no human
knows exactly how all of it works. It's
impossible to even read all of the code.

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:35.020
Even if it were freely available, it would
be impossible to read all of it and
understand it for a certain human being.

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:42.400
Yet, it kind of sort of works. That same
laptop interacts, as I said, with every
other. It can interact with lots of other

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:50.700
computers in the world. And it works. So
how do you build something out of parts
that you kind of sort of understand, but

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:59.180
you certainly do not understand the whole
thing? So most of the time, we do not
understand the entire system. We cannot.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:12.340
It's just too complicated. But we need to
navigate that uncertainty and figure it
out anyway. So in this course, the obvious

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:20.660
thing we'll learn is what are computer
networks? How exactly does it function?
But then we want to go one level meta and

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:26.360
see why are the systems designed this way?
What would have happened had they been
designed another way? What are exactly the

00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:35.440
trade-offs between the various means we
could design them? And that is the deeper
question we can ask. And the even deeper

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:46.040
question, which is really what I would
like you to take away from courses like
this, is how do you design systems in

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:53.420
general? We'll be taking the example of
computer networks, but the same principles
apply not just across all other types of

00:09:53.420 --> 00:10:03.540
computer systems, but all other types of
systems. If you talk to someone who's
designing cars, there is a lot of

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:08.320
recognizable language. There's a lot of
the same complexities that we deal with.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:17.400
Because only some small fraction of you
will actually work on computer networks
going forward. But almost all of you will

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:24.200
probably have something to deal with
systems in general, broadly speaking, even
if you leave computer science.

