00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:55.360
So what does the assignment look like? So
what we've built is a network simulator.

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:07.100
The networks are built up of things called
switches, which I have not talked about,
but the Wi-Fi router is a switch, for

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:15.000
example. And you are going to write a
program for that to get installed on all
of those switches.

00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:27.100
And the first three assignments are more
classical. There are some things that your
implementation must do. First assignment,

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:33.040
you just have to get the packets across.
Second assignment, you have to get as many
packets across as possible. It has to

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:39.360
perform well. Third assignment, you want
to understand how the traffic looks, who's
sending packets to whom, so that you know

00:21:39.360 --> 00:21:44.580
how to better service your users. The next
two assignments are more fun.

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:55.740
So each of you will control a subnetwork.
It's called an autonomous system, is what
we call it in network parlance, AS.

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:09.040
So each of your code will be installed on
the switches that you control. And then
your job is to provide the best

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:18.380
performance and the best user service that
you can. Users are simulated agents. And
each agent will have a different

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:25.100
application. One will be a streaming
service like Netrix. They need a lot of
bandwidth. They don't care as much about

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:34.640
latency. Another will be like Cloudflare.
They're basically serving ordinary
websites. A third will be some sort of a

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:44.940
gaming provider who requires really low
latency all the time. Another will be a
high-frequency trading firm. And high

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:51.840
-frequency traders really care about ultra
-low latency. But that's a bit of a
misunderstanding. It's not that they care

00:22:51.840 --> 00:23:01.240
about low latency. They care about getting
a lower latency than the other guy. So for
instance, there's a trading center in

00:23:01.240 --> 00:23:07.020
Chicago and one in New York. And they
really care that whatever happens in
Chicago immediately comes to New York as

00:23:07.020 --> 00:23:11.100
quickly as possible, faster than the other
people, so that they can take advantage of
that information.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:22.560
And each of them is going to evaluate
performance differently, depending on what
their application wants. And they're going

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:28.620
to pick the best autonomous system. And
they're going to pay you money. So the
better for service you provide, the more

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:34.040
money you can charge. Ultimately, it's
like Monopoly. You have to end up making
the most amount of, I mean, it's not real

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:34.700
money. It's fake money.

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:46.000
And the reason we are OK with you using
LLMs for this is first, for the first
three assignments,

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:56.240
you can use LLMs to write 100% of the
code. But you still need to know what that
code does. So in the quizzes, we will ask

00:23:56.240 --> 00:24:05.720
you, if we gave your program this input,
what happens? And we will take that answer
by running your own code and seeing what

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:15.580
happens. So there will be two types of
questions. One is, this is the exact
input. What does your program do? The

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:26.360
second will be a meta-property. Among all
possible things we can do, like does your
routing algorithm, what is the maximum

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:34.560
time your routing algorithm takes to
converge? Or what is the maximum
efficiency you can achieve? Something like

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:41.780
that. For all possible ways things can
happen, what does your program do? And
we'll actually test that against your real

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:51.700
code. Second, so you don't need to know
the lines of your code, but you need to
understand what it does.

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:59.460
For the second part, like the last two
assignments, we have to be confident that
the LLMs do not know the answer to those

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:06.060
questions. You could post all of your
solutions on GitHub. Next year, we'll
still be confident that the LLMs do not

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:09.500
know the answer to those questions.
Because first, nobody knows the answer.

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:18.680
We understand how each component of the
internet works, that does not mean we
understand how the internet actually

00:25:18.680 --> 00:25:29.720
works. And this is true not just for the
internet, but any complex system. So the
ocean of what we do not know is much

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:37.060
larger than the tiny rocks of what we do
know. They're not even islands, tiny
little rocks. And the second reason we are

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:49.000
confident that the LLMs don't know the
answer is because there is no answer. What
I mean by that is the best answer depends

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:57.360
on what the other students are doing. If
everybody says, I want the HFT market,
then only one or two people are going to

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:05.340
get the HFT market, or they're going to
non-compete on the prices like crazy. So
you have to specialize your network in a

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:11.720
way that is complementary to how others
are specializing them. So you really have
to understand the design space. You have

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:21.340
to make a really high quality autonomous
system design, and you want to understand
what the customers want, how others are

00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:28.320
handling it, and which part of the market
you need to capture. So this is high level
reasoning that we believe so far humans

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:37.480
are still better than LLMs at. If you
just, the first reason is if everybody
takes the standard templated floor answer,

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:46.040
then the one person who thinks and does
something different will make vastly more
money. And second, LLMs are great at

00:26:46.040 --> 00:26:52.320
implementing stuff. You say, do this,
they'll go do that, and they'll probably
work in the first round. They'll do some

00:26:52.320 --> 00:27:00.260
bugs, but you can figure it out. But when
they have to stitch together multiple high
level ideas, they're not that good.

00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:08.220
They're going to give you standard
templated ideas that are going to sound
very convincing, but are not very smart.

