So we've discussed this. If the input rate
exceeds the output rate, that's exactly
when two people want to send to the same

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:59.560
output queue. So the benefit of cut
-through switching is you get slightly
lower latency, because you don't have to

00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:09.700
store first and then forward. But the cost
of that lower latency is enormous
complexity. As we discussed in the last

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:20.580
class, sometimes if you're literally
shining lasers, then a cut-through switch
is simply a mirror that's bouncing off the

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:22.700
laser. So it's very easy to implement.

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:35.820
And if cut-through switching is giving you
that enormous ease in implementation, then
you accept the resulting complexity

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:38.900
overhead. But usually, you don't accept
that kind of complexity.

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:49.660
And there are other ways to provide
reliability other than just cut-through
switching. Don't equate cut-through

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:57.360
switching with non-best-effort networks.
But I do not think we will be discussing
that in this course.

